Q1. What is User Federation in Keycloak?

Plain: Keycloak doesn’t keep its own list of people. It “borrows” them from the directory and asks the directory to check passwords.
Technical: a federation provider connects a realm to an external user store (LDAP, AD, or a custom provider). Keycloak looks users up there and validates credentials against it. Your realm conote has the conote-openldap provider. Every student01 login is checked by an LDAP bind.

Q2. Importing vs federating users?

Federate only (import off): no local copy, and every lookup goes live to LDAP. It’s always current, but LDAP must be reachable for everything, and there’s nowhere to attach Keycloak-only data.
Import: Keycloak stores a linked copy of the profile, refreshed by sync. Passwords are still checked by LDAP. Keycloak-only data (roles, MFA, sessions, linked accounts) has a place to live.
Your choice (D-16): import + READ_ONLY + 60-second changed-user sync. C1 showed the trade-off: email waited for the sync, while department, set to always read from LDAP, was instant.

Q3. What is an LDAP bind account?

Plain: the service’s own login to the directory, used only to search for people and read their details.
Technical: it’s a DN and password that Keycloak uses for its directory connection. Yours is cn=keycloak-bind,ou=services. It’s read-only by ACL, can’t read password hashes, and isn’t a person, so it never appears as a user. You never use cn=admin: a leaked federation configuration must not hand over the whole directory (D-15, D-17).

Q4. What happens when a user is disabled in the source directory?

New logins fail immediately. Every login is an LDAP bind, and the locked account’s bind is refused, even with the right password (M2-12, M2-13).
Keycloak’s local copy still shows “Enabled”, because the lock is an LDAP password-policy attribute that the default mappers don’t read. Login is still denied.
Existing sessions and tokens keep working until they expire. So the full off-boarding procedure is: disable in LDAP, then sign the user out in Keycloak and revoke their sessions. Short token lifetimes (M7) limit the gap.
With Active Directory instead, Keycloak reads the userAccountControl flag and marks the user disabled itself.

Q5. Why must the identity source remain authoritative for institutional accounts?

Plain: the registry office decides who is a student. If each system kept its own list, someone who left could still get in through the system nobody updated.
Technical: one source of truth means:
joiners and leavers are handled once, by the right office (registry or HR);
there’s no drift or conflict between copies;
one audit trail;
one place to disable an account.
READ_ONLY mode enforces this technically: Keycloak can’t change directory data (C1 bonus check).
